# LEVEL 3 — INTERVIEW + DOUBT CLEARING

## 8. Why does `compareTo()` return `int`?

Because Java needs to represent three ordering conditions:

```text
Current < Other
Current = Other
Current > Other
```

Therefore:

```text
Negative
Zero
Positive
```

An `int` can represent all three.

---

# 9. Do we have to return exactly -1, 0 and 1?

**No.**

The sign is what matters.

For example:

```text
-10 → BEFORE
-2  → BEFORE
-1  → BEFORE

0   → SAME

1   → AFTER
10  → AFTER
100 → AFTER
```

So:

> The exact non-zero value is not important; its sign is important.

---

# 10. Does Comparable perform sorting?

**No.**

This is one of the most important distinctions.

```text
Comparable
    ↓
Defines comparison rule

compareTo()
    ↓
Defines relative ordering

Collections.sort()
    ↓
Actually performs sorting
```

So:

> **Comparable tells Java how to order objects; the sorting operation uses that rule.**

---

# 11. What does "Natural Ordering" really mean?

Suppose we have:

```java
class Employee
{
    int id;
    String name;
    double salary;
}
```

We could order employees by:

```text
ID
Name
Salary
```

If we decide:

```text
Employee → ID
```

and implement `compareTo()` using `id`, then:

```text
ID
↓
Natural Ordering
```

So:

> Natural ordering is the default ordering that the class itself defines.

---

# 12. Can a class have different sorting requirements?

Yes.

Suppose Student has:

```text
rollNo
name
marks
```

We may want:

```text
Sort by rollNo
Sort by name
Sort by marks
```

`Comparable` is normally used for the **natural/default ordering**.

For alternative/custom ordering, we use:

```text
Comparator
```

Therefore:

```text
Comparable
    ↓
Natural / Default ordering

Comparator
    ↓
Custom / Alternative ordering
```

You will study `Comparator` separately.

---

# 13. Comparable vs `compareTo()`

Don't confuse them.

### Comparable

An interface:

```java
Comparable
```

### `compareTo()`

The comparison method:

```java
compareTo()
```

Relationship:

```text
Comparable
     ↓
contains/defines compareTo()
     ↓
comparison logic
```

---

# 14. Comparable vs Natural Ordering

These are also not exactly the same thing.

| Comparable             | Natural Ordering          |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Interface              | Concept/order             |
| Provides `compareTo()` | Default ordering          |
| Mechanism              | Result of comparison rule |
| Implemented by a class | Defined by the class      |

Easy memory:

```text
Comparable = MECHANISM

Natural Ordering = DEFAULT ORDER
```

---

# 15. `compareTo()` vs `equals()`

Another common interview doubt.

### `equals()`

Asks:

> Are these objects equal?

### `compareTo()`

Asks:

> Which object comes first?

```text
equals()
    ↓
Equal / Not Equal

compareTo()
    ↓
Before / Same ordering / After
```

Also remember:

```java
compareTo() == 0
```

means the objects are considered equal **for ordering purposes**.

It does not universally guarantee:

```java
equals() == true
```

---

# 16. Why do we cast `Object`?

Because our training examples are deliberately avoiding Generics.

We write:

```java
public int compareTo(Object obj)
```

So `obj` is an `Object`.

We need:

```java
Student s = (Student)obj;
```

to access Student-specific members:

```java
s.rollNo
```

The flow is:

```text
Object
  ↓
type cast
  ↓
Student
  ↓
access rollNo
```

---

# 17. Most Important Code to Remember

```java
class Student implements Comparable
{
    int rollNo;

    public int compareTo(Object obj)
    {
        Student s = (Student)obj;

        return this.rollNo - s.rollNo;
    }
}
```

Understand this as:

```text
Student implements Comparable
        ↓
Student can have natural ordering

compareTo()
        ↓
compares two Students

this.rollNo - s.rollNo
        ↓
